## Why this should help

- The refinement directly attacks the weakness of greedy Steiner construction: suboptimal attachment edges chosen early. Deleting an edge and reconnecting via the cheapest path from the surviving tree can only reduce the distinct-edge cost, and the loop runs until no improving swap exists.
- The extra rotation orders are nearly free (graphs are small) and give the refiner more diverse starting points, increasing the chance one lands in a better basin.
- Runtime remains far below the 600s limit: each refine pass is O(|E_tree| × Dijkstra), tiny for region-level graphs.
- Success behavior is unchanged (same path emission logic, same fallbacks), so success_rate should stay at 1.0 while total_cost drops, improving cost_score and thus combined_score.
